@vsm 1.0
// nucleus_realdata_wiring.spec.vsm — wire Component A to real stasis_cos data + emit real orders
//
// Author: Claude (S3-S5 role, this session on Sonnet 5), 2026-07-27, per rmw3 direct steer:
// "lo primero es asegurar que el propio nucleo pueda manejar este proceso, aun sin clima" —
// prove the NUCLEUS's own mechanism is sound first; real clima L3->L2 integration is a
// separate, later, deferred task. This spec is scoped to exactly that split.
//
// Context: while preparing to execute go_live_runbook.vsm (rmw3 authorized go_live_execution),
// direct investigation on n02/LXC 101/LXC 112 found 3 real gaps, not the 1 originally assumed:
//   1. cgroup cpu delegation for the 8 clima L2 zones was never enabled — FIXED same session,
//      verified live (sum=800, systemctl + direct cgroup-file reads agree). Out of this spec's
//      scope — already closed.
//   2. Component B's read_projection_stasis_cos() expects {zones:[{name,current_sc}]}; the REAL
//      clima projection file produces {projections:{<L3-station>:{current_sc,...}}} — wrong key,
//      wrong granularity (20 L3 stations, not the 8 L2 zones Component A/B actually manage).
//   3. THE real gap: Component A's own production stasis_cos floor is never populated with real
//      data. grep of s3_anticipation.rs confirms set_zone_stasis_cos() is called ONLY inside
//      #[test] functions (lines ~1290-1349) — never from start_directive_subscriber or any
//      production code path. select_donor's own fallback when stasis_cos is absent is "allow"
//      (graceful degradation, meant for tolerable async lag) — but since nothing ever populates
//      it in production, this fallback is PERMANENT, meaning the R2/gap_1 viability floor that
//      tonight's production_hardening_gate.spec.vsm certified as "closed" is certified against
//      unit tests with hand-fed data, not against anything that runs in the live kernel. Same
//      fail-open shape as the bbh_policy_gate.py bug fixed earlier this same session (unparsed/
//      absent data treated as permissive instead of restrictive) — recognized pattern, not a
//      coincidence, fix the same way: fail-closed.

spec(NUCLEUS-REALDATA-WIRING) {

  why: "go_live_runbook.vsm's flip would have looked successful (kernel logs 'PRODUCTION mode',
      REAL-tagged grant events, tests still green) while the actual safety floor it exists to
      enforce silently did nothing, because no real data ever reaches it. This is exactly the
      'looks real, isn't' failure mode this project's rigor protocol exists to catch — caught
      before the flip, not after, because Claude investigated the live cgroup/projection state
      directly instead of trusting the runbook's own preflight checklist (which itself checks the
      WRONG field name — see gap 2 below).",

  scope_boundary: "This spec proves the NUCLEUS's own mechanism (read projection -> populate real
      stasis_cos -> make a correct donor decision -> emit a real, correctly-formatted order file)
      is sound, using a nucleus-controlled/fixture projection file in the AGREED schema. It does
      NOT attempt to solve clima's real L3-station -> L2-zone aggregation — that remains a
      separate, later, clima-side (or DS-authored aggregation script) task, explicitly deferred
      per rmw3's steer. Once this spec's mechanism is proven, wiring a real clima aggregator to
      PRODUCE a file in the agreed schema is a drop-in replacement for the fixture — the nucleus
      code does not need to change again for that step.",

  // ── THE CANONICAL CONTRACT — formalize what was previously only implicit ──────────
  nucleus_projection_contract: {
    what: "The schema Component A's own tests and Component B's read_projection_stasis_cos()
        ALREADY independently agree on — this spec makes it an explicit, documented contract
        instead of an implicit assumption two different pieces of code happened to share.",
    format: "{ ts: RFC3339, zones: [ {name: <one of the 8 L2 zone names>, current_sc: float}, ... ] }",
    path: "/var/lib/vsf/vsf_nucleus/s4_markov_projection.json on LXC 101 (SAME path both
        components already reference — no new path introduced). Whatever eventually produces
        this file for real (a clima-side L3->L2 aggregator, built later, out of this spec's
        scope) must write in exactly this shape. Until then, a fixture file in this shape is
        sufficient to prove the nucleus's mechanism.",
    not_this_spec: "Component B's read_projection_stasis_cos() code does not need to change —
        it already expects this exact contract. What needs to change is Component A (never
        reads any projection file for real) and, later/separately, whatever produces the real
        file (currently produces a different, L3-keyed schema — clima-side problem).",
  },

  // ── FIX 1 — fail-closed correction ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  fail_closed_correction: {
    finding: "select_donor (s3_anticipation.rs ~line 221-223): when self.stasis_cos.get(*name)
        returns None (zone not yet populated), the filter falls through to `true` (donor
        allowed). Comment calls this 'graceful degradation — data may arrive asynchronously' —
        a reasonable design FOR TRANSIENT lag, but since nothing populates the map in
        production today, this is the PERMANENT behavior, not a transient one.",
    fix: "In production mode (!self.synthetic), a zone with no stasis_cos entry — OR a stale
        one past a freshness threshold, see next section — must be EXCLUDED from donor
        selection, not allowed. Missing/stale real-world safety data must never read as
        permission. Same principle as this session's bbh_policy_gate.py fix
        (prohibited_methods_status=unparsed -> fail-closed, not ALLOW-with-warning) — cite
        that fix directly in the commit message, this is the same bug class recurring.",
    caveat: "This makes the very FIRST grant after a kernel restart or projection outage
        excellent at doing nothing (no zones populated yet -> no eligible donors -> D4
        double-loop escalation) rather than silently permissive. That is the CORRECT
        conservative default for a safety floor — a temporary inability to grant is a much
        smaller failure than a silent, permanent bypass of the floor. Confirm the double-loop
        escalation path (already built, tested) handles a fully-empty-donor-pool cleanly — it
        should, per the existing 'No donor found -> D4 double-loop' path in select_donor,
        but re-run that specific test after this change to confirm.",
  },

  // ── FIX 2 — component A real-data wiring ──────────────────────────────────────────
  component_a_changes: {
    new_function: "fn refresh_stasis_cos(&mut self, projection_path: &str) -> Result<(), String>
        — reads the file at nucleus_projection_contract's path+format, parses it, and calls
        self.set_zone_stasis_cos(name, current_sc) for each of the 8 known L2 zone names found
        in the file's zones list. Unknown zone names in the file (not one of the 8) are ignored,
        logged at debug level, not an error — the nucleus only cares about its own 8 zones.",
    freshness: "Track the file's mtime (or its own 'ts' field) same as Component B's
        STALE_PROJECTION_SECS=600 pattern — do not blindly trust a stale read. If the file is
        stale beyond a threshold (recommend matching Component B's own 600s for consistency, a
        deliberate shared constant, not a coincidence), treat every zone as unpopulated for
        THIS cycle (do not use last-known-good silently — see fail_closed_correction, staleness
        is a form of absence, not a form of presence).",
    call_site: "start_directive_subscriber's main loop (the same loop that already
        polls/subscribes for directives) — call refresh_stasis_cos() once per loop iteration
        (or on a slower cadence than the directive poll if that's cheaper — DS's call, note the
        reasoning) BEFORE calling handle_directive, so every directive is evaluated against the
        freshest available data for that cycle.",
    error_handling: "A read/parse failure (file missing, malformed JSON) must NOT crash the
        subscriber loop — log the error, treat as 'no fresh data this cycle' (same fail-closed
        rule above), continue running. The kernel staying up matters more than any single
        cycle's data freshness.",
  },

  // ── FIX 3 — order-file emission (component A -> component B) ─────────────────────
  order_emission: {
    what: "After prepare_grant_meta + emit_grant_event (handle_directive's existing step 4/5),
        ALSO write an order file to the bind-mount directory Component B watches, in EXACTLY
        the order_format component_b_host_reallocator.spec.vsm already defines (directive_id,
        ts, donor_zone, target_zone, delta, kernel_claimed_donor_stasis_cos,
        kernel_claimed_sum_after). Component B's Order.load() already parses this shape —
        no changes needed on Component B's side for this fix.",
    when: "Emit in BOTH synthetic and production mode. Rationale: this is what makes true
        shadow-first integration testing possible — Component B can run in its existing
        --dry-run mode against REAL orders from a synthetic-mode Component A, proving the full
        pipeline end-to-end with zero actuation risk, before production mode or real cgroup
        writes are ever considered. Add a `kernel_mode: \"synthetic\"|\"production\"` field to
        the order (Component B's order_format doesn't currently have this — small addition,
        confirm with Component B's Order class that an extra field is ignored gracefully, not
        an error, before relying on it).",
    path: "Same bind-mount directory named in component_b_host_reallocator.spec.vsm
        (host_dir=/var/lib/vsf-anticipation/orders/, LXC 101 side via a NEW rw bind-mount —
        this mount does not exist yet either, it is part of this spec's deployment_prep below).",
  },

  // ── DEPLOYMENT PREP (still shadow-only — no real actuation) ───────────────────────
  deployment_prep: {
    bind_mount: "LXC 101 needs `pct set 101 -mp0 /var/lib/vsf-anticipation/orders,mp=/mnt/
        anticipation-orders,ro=0` (or the next free mpN slot) — rw per Component A needing to
        WRITE orders there. This is a real infra change (container config + restart) — flag it
        for explicit confirmation before applying, same as the cgroup delegation fix was
        confirmed first. Directory /var/lib/vsf-anticipation/orders/{pending,done,rejected}
        needs creating on the n02 host first (mkdir -p, owned appropriately for LXC 101's
        unprivileged UID range to write into it — check LXC 101's own privilege mode before
        assuming a simple mkdir is sufficient, same class of gotcha as LXC 112 being
        unprivileged this session).",
    still_not_authorized: "Component B is NOT deployed to the host in this spec. No systemd
        timer for vsf_cgroup_reallocate.py is installed. No real cgroup writes happen — even
        with the bind-mount and order-emission wired, nothing reads orders/pending/ on the host
        side yet unless Component B is explicitly deployed, which remains a separate,
        later-authorized step per TASKS.vsm(COMPONENT-B)'s own done_when/status_note.",
  },

  // ── INTEGRATION TEST — proves the mechanism, still entirely isolated ─────────────
  integration_test_plan: {
    step_1: "Write a fixture projection file matching nucleus_projection_contract with a
        realistic mix (some zones healthy, at least one below BASIN_FLOOR=0.875, at least one
        missing entirely from the file to exercise the fail-closed path).",
    step_2: "Run Component A (in a test harness, or the real binary pointed at the fixture path
        via env var / CLI arg — whichever is less invasive to add) through a directive that
        would naturally select the below-floor zone as donor if the floor were not enforced.
        Assert: that zone is excluded, a healthy zone is chosen instead, OR (if no healthy donor
        has enough slack) a D4 double-loop escalation occurs — never a silent grant from the
        unhealthy zone.",
    step_3: "Assert an order file was written to orders/pending/ (a temp dir standing in for the
        real bind-mount in this test) in the exact shape Component B's Order.load() parses
        without error — literally import vsf_cgroup_reallocate.py's Order class (or shell out
        to it) and load the file Component A wrote, prove no exception, all fields present and
        correctly typed. This is the actual proof the two components speak the same language,
        not an assumption.",
    step_4: "Run that loaded order through Component B's real validate_order() (not a mock) with
        Component B's OWN independent cgroup/projection readers pointed at the same fixture
        data — confirm validate_order's pass/fail decision matches what a human reading the
        fixture data would expect. This is the actual end-to-end proof, still zero real
        actuation (never call actuate_order in this test, or call it with dry_run=True only).",
    step_5: "Re-run the existing 18 s3_anticipation tests + 15 Component B tests — confirm
        nothing regressed.",
  },

  boundary: "Everything in this spec is build+test in isolation, matching every other
      deliverable tonight. Explicitly NOT authorized by this spec: the bind-mount's actual
      creation on live LXC 101 (flag for confirmation first, per deployment_prep), Component B's
      host deployment, any real cgroup write, and the go_live_runbook.vsm flip itself. All of
      those remain gated behind explicit rmw3 (S5) decisions, same boundary as
      component_b_host_reallocator.spec.vsm and TASKS.vsm(COMPONENT-B).",

  next: "Dispatch to DS: implement fail_closed_correction + component_a_changes + order_emission
      + the integration_test_plan, entirely in the existing test/fixture harness (no live LXC 101
      changes). Report back before deployment_prep's bind-mount is created — that specific step
      needs its own explicit go-ahead since it changes a live container's config.",
}
